  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>When the force&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is removed, the particle starts accelerating because of the resultant force between&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>.</p><p>The resultant force can be calculated as follows:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable columnalign=\"right center left\" columnspacing=\"0px\"><mtr><mtd><mi>F</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><msqrt><msup><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></msup></msqrt></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p><p>Hence, the acceleration of the particle is given by</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable columnspacing=\"0px\" columnalign=\"right center left\"><mtr><mtd><mi>a</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi></mrow></mfrac></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd/><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>&#8211;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p>",
